---
/**
 * The download page (U6: R10-R14).
 *
 * Shares the `doc` layout with the guide so the two read as one set of
 * documents. The SmartScreen warning is stated up front, at full weight, with
 * the reason and the steps - burying it would get more installs and fewer
 * informed ones, and the people this is for are exactly the people who heed
 * that warning.
 */
import { readFileSync } from 'node:fs'

import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import Callout from '../components/Callout.astro'
import Steps from '../components/Steps.astro'
import { DOWNLOAD_URL, httpFetchers, latestRelease, REPO } from '../lib/release'

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL

// The app's own declared version is the fallback when the API is unreachable.
const conf = JSON.parse(
  readFileSync(new URL('../../../src-tauri/tauri.conf.json', import.meta.url), 'utf8'),
)
const release = await latestRelease(httpFetchers(process.env.GITHUB_TOKEN), conf.version)

const published = release.publishedAt
  ? new Date(release.publishedAt).toLocaleDateString('en-GB', {
      day: 'numeric',
      month: 'long',
      year: 'numeric',
    })
  : null

const sections = [
  ['warning', 'The Windows warning'],
  ['verify', 'Verify your download'],
  ['requirements', 'What you need'],
  ['limits', 'Before you trust it'],
]
---

<Base
  title="Download Sanctum for Windows"
  description="Sanctum for Windows 10 and 11. Verify the checksum before you install, and read why Windows will warn you."
>
  <div class="doc wrap">
    <aside class="doc__rail">
      <a class="backLink" href={base}>&larr; Landing page</a>
      <p class="doc__railLabel">On this page</p>
      <ol class="doc__toc">
        {sections.map(([id, label]) => (
          <li>
            <a href={`#${id}`}>{label}</a>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ol>
    </aside>

    <article class="doc__body">
      <p class="eyebrow">Windows 10 and 11 &middot; 64-bit</p>
      <h1>Download Sanctum</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Version {release.version}{published && <> &middot; published {published}</>}. Around
        3&nbsp;MB.
        {!release.live && (
          <> This page could not reach GitHub, so the version shown is the one this build declares.</>
        )}
      </p>

      <div class="heroActions dlActions">
        <a class="btn btn-primary btn--lg" href={DOWNLOAD_URL} rel="noopener">
          Download Sanctum {release.version}
        </a>
        <a class="btn" href={`https://github.com/${REPO}/releases`} rel="noopener">All releases</a>
      </div>

      <h2 id="warning">The Windows warning</h2>
      <p>
        This installer is not code-signed. Windows SmartScreen shows
        <em>&ldquo;Windows protected your PC&rdquo;</em> and hides the Run button behind
        <em>More info</em>.
      </p>
      <p>
        That warning is not a false alarm. It is Windows telling you truthfully that it cannot
        identify who published this file. A code-signing certificate costs a few hundred dollars a
        year, Sanctum does not have one yet, and the honest thing is to say so rather than talk you
        past it.
      </p>

      <Steps steps={[
        'Verify the checksum below before running anything. This is the step that actually protects you.',
        'Run the installer. When SmartScreen appears, click <strong>More info</strong>.',
        'Click <strong>Run anyway</strong>.',
      ]} />

      <Callout kind="note" title="What signing would and would not change">
        <p>
          A certificate proves the publisher is who they claim, and lets Windows build reputation
          across releases. It does not vouch for the code, and it does not make an early release
          trustworthy on its own &mdash; a newly signed application still triggers warnings until
          enough people have installed it.
        </p>
        <p>
          Signing is planned. Until then, the checksum is the real verification, and reading the
          source is the real assurance.
        </p>
      </Callout>

      <h2 id="verify">Verify your download</h2>
      {release.sha256 ? (
        <>
          <p>The SHA-256 of the installer published with this release:</p>
          <p class="hash"><code>{release.sha256}</code></p>
          <p>Check yours matches, in PowerShell:</p>
          <pre class="cmd"><code>Get-FileHash .\Sanctum_{release.version}_x64-setup.exe -Algorithm SHA256</code></pre>
          <p>
            If the hashes differ, do not run the file. Either the download was corrupted, or it is
            not the file that was built.
          </p>
        </>
      ) : (
        <p>
          No checksum has been published for this release yet. When one exists it appears as a
          <code>SHA256SUMS.txt</code> asset on the
          <a href={`https://github.com/${REPO}/releases`}> release page</a>, and here.
        </p>
      )}

      <h2 id="requirements">What you need</h2>
      <ul class="plainList">
        <li><strong>Windows 10 or 11</strong>, 64-bit. There is no 32-bit or ARM build.</li>
        <li>
          <strong>WebView2</strong>, which ships with Windows 11. On Windows 10 the installer
          fetches it automatically if it is missing &mdash; the only network request anything here
          makes.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>About 20&nbsp;MB of disk</strong> once installed. Your vault grows with what you
          put in it.
        </li>
        <li>No administrator rights. It installs for the current user.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="limits">Before you trust it with anything</h2>
      <div class="twoUp">
        <article class="tile">
          <h3>Where it stands</h3>
          <ul class="plainList">
            <li>Version 0.1.0. Early, and honest about it.</li>
            <li>Not independently audited.</li>
            <li>Not code-signed.</li>
            <li>Windows only. No macOS or Linux build.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>
        <article class="tile tile--warn">
          <h3>What you are taking on</h3>
          <ul class="plainList">
            <li>No sync and no cloud copy. Take a backup and check you can restore it.</li>
            <li>
              Lose your passphrase <em>and</em> your recovery code and the data is gone. No reset,
              no email, no backdoor.
            </li>
            <li>
              An unlocked vault on a compromised machine is readable. No desktop vault solves that.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>
      </div>
      <p>
        The <a href={`${base}guide/`}>guide</a> covers all of this properly, and the
        <a href={`https://github.com/${REPO}`}> source</a> is readable &mdash; which is the only
        assurance available until an audit exists.
      </p>

      <nav class="doc__nav">
        <a class="doc__navLink" href={`${base}guide/`}>
          <span class="doc__navLabel">Before you install</span>
          Read the guide
        </a>
        <a class="doc__navLink doc__navLink--next" href={base}>
          <span class="doc__navLabel">Back to</span>
          Landing page
        </a>
      </nav>
    </article>
  </div>
</Base>
